                <pb facs="0163" n="147" sameAs="frus:frus1951v07p1/pg147"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Editorial Note</hi>
                </head>
                <p>On January 30, at the conclusion of two days of meetings in Washington, President
                    Truman and French Prime Minister René Pleven issued a joint statement, the text
                    of which is printed in <hi rendition="#italic">Public Papers of the Presidents
                        of the United States: Harry S. Truman, 1951</hi>, page 128. Their
                    discussions dealt with problems relating to the Far East, principally Indochina,
                    Europe, Atlantic defense, and economic matters. Concerning Korea, the joint
                    statement expressed agreement on the need for continued resistance to aggression
                    until an honorable solution could be found, as well as the need for preventing
                    hostilities <pb facs="0164" n="148" xml:id="pg148"/> from spreading beyond
                    Korea. For full documentation on the Franco-American talks of January 29–30, see
                    the compilation on France in volume IV.</p>
            </div>
